<p>In 1883, a small house owned by {% person "charles-f-king" %}Charles F.{% endperson %} and {% person "estella-king" %}Estella King{% endperson %} stood on lots four, five, and six along Third Street NW. Dr. King served as Morton County superintendent of schools from 1883 to 1885. Estella was the daughter of {% person "isaac-cummins" %}Isaac Cummins{% endperson %}, a telegraph operator for the Northern Pacific Railway and Western Union Telegraph Company.</p>

<p>The Kings lived there with their children—Lucille, Otis, and Lila—Charles's mother, Liza Ann, and their domestic worker, Marion Habberstad.</p>

<p>{% person "james-henry-mcgillic" %}James Henry McGillic{% endperson %} and {% person "john-orourke" %}John O'Rourke{% endperson %} came to Mandan from Malone, New York, and opened the O'Rourke and McGillic grocery and meat market in 1879.</p>

<p>Three years later, a fire that began in the Northern Pacific restaurant destroyed their store and many other buildings. It remains the most destructive fire in Mandan's history by number of buildings lost.</p>

