<p>McGillic purchased the residential property in 1913 and sold its western third. In 1916, he rebuilt the structure, creating the present house on the eastern half of lots five and six. The 33-by-38-foot building has 18-foot studs and cost approximately {% inflation 4000, 1916 %} to construct.</p>

<p>The two-and-a-half-story house is built in the Craftsman style popular from 1905 to 1930. It has a large front-gabled dormer, a second-floor balcony over the porch, and a full-width porch with square columns supporting the roof. The house contains four bedrooms, one full bathroom, and two half-bathrooms.</p>

<p>The interior included servants' quarters and a separate staircase providing household staff access to the kitchen. Quarter-sawn oak is used extensively throughout the main floor in the woodwork, wainscoting, and built-in china cabinet.</p>

<p>The builders' signatures remain visible in the woodwork of the attic, which has an eight-foot ceiling.</p>
